 ## Context
 
-Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest.
+Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest. The kiosk has no network route, so every lookup is answered from files on the kiosk.
 
 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed. The certificates node uses only this interface and never reads registry storage.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookup is served by a local embedded SQLite index that each kiosk builds from the snapshot it imports. The index file, its schema, and its location are private to this node.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+1. Verify the signature using a bundled public key.
+2. Reject unsupported schema versions.
+3. Validate record serial numbers for uniqueness.
+4. Build a new SQLite index from the records in a staging location, separate from the active index.
+5. Activate the new index only after steps 1–4 succeed, by a single atomic switch of which index is active.
+
+Records are never readable from the staging index. An import failure at any step discards the staging index, leaves the previous snapshot active, and reports which step failed and why.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters, then query the active index by serial number. The same normalization is applied to serial numbers when the index is built, so stored and queried forms agree. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index built on the kiosk | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Lookup stays on kiosk files, as the HLD tenets require. Both alternatives need runtime network access, which kiosks do not have. SQLite lookups are predictable and need no service. The cost is that each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index when a snapshot arrives; that cost stays inside this node. |
+| Index activation | Build in staging, then atomic switch | Rebuild the active index in place | The previous snapshot must stay available if import fails, and partially imported records must never be visible. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Uniqueness comparison: whether two serials that differ only by case or surrounding whitespace count as duplicates at import (see REG-003).
+2. Behavior at startup when no valid snapshot has ever been imported.
+3. Behavior when the active index fails an integrity check at startup.
+4. Free-space requirement and check for staging an index alongside the active one, given snapshots up to 150 MB.
